{
  "dom_id": "step-q103539",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}$ at the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$",
